The FIX (one name per compilation unit, or detect a parse-class diagnostic and
re-run the batch name-by-name) needs no game. **The gate does.** Both the
regression test and step 2 of the Work section — re-probing the shipped
`b052`/`b053` ACCEPTED rows to find out whether any are corrupted — require a
live RemoteBuild on 127.0.0.1:30000.

Trackmania exited on 2026-08-30 during the B067 campaign and has not been
restarted; doing so puts a window on the user's desktop, so it waits for them.

Do not start this ticket expecting to finish it. When the game is back:
re-probe the 742 ACCEPTED rows of `b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv` in
isolation, diff, and report the flip count before touching anything else — that
number decides whether this is a tooling fix or a corpus regeneration that
B052/B056/B057 all need to hear about.

## Exposure to the SHIPPED corpora is bounded — and it is the mild direction
## (lane-b057, 2026-08-31; reasoning checked by coordinator)

Corpus sizes: `b052` = **1159 REJECTED / 742 ACCEPTED**; `b053` = **2373
NO_SYMBOL / 592 ACCEPTED**.

**This defect can only manufacture a spurious ACCEPTED.** A REJECTED row
required the game to emit a real diagnostic naming that spelling; a parse abort
produces the *absence* of diagnostics, never their presence. So no REJECTED row
can be an artefact of B068, and every "the LSP must reject this" assertion
built on the corpora is sound.

Consequences, which lower this ticket's severity:

- **B056 is robust.** Its headline claim — the LSP accepted 1010 spellings the
  game rejects — is a narrowing built entirely on REJECTED rows.
- **The risk runs the other way.** A spurious ACCEPTED tells the LSP to accept
  something the game may reject, i.e. B056 possibly narrowed too *little*.
  That is a missed error (false negative), not a false positive. **The
  damaging direction is not exposed.**

This does not make the fix optional — a corpus that can silently invent
ACCEPTED rows will eventually be used for a claim that leans on them, and the
harness stays wrong until fixed. But it does mean this is not a fire, nothing
already merged needs revisiting on its account, and the re-probe (Work step 2)
is a confirmation rather than a rescue.

Anything that DOES lean on an ACCEPTED row should say so explicitly, the way
`d51fc32` does after moving the canonicalization fence off three of them.